	project:convert:mdapi # To use Salesforce CLI to work with components that you retrieved via Metadata API, first convert your files from the metadata format to the source format using this command.  To convert files from the source format back to the metadata format, run "sf project convert source".  To convert multiple metadata components, either set multiple --metadata <name> flags or a single --metadata flag with multiple names separated by spaces.
		--api-version # Override the api version used for api requests made by this command
		--flags-dir # Import flag values from a directory.
		--json @bool # Format output as json.
		--loglevel
		--manifest|-x # If you specify this flag, don’t specify --metadata or --source-dir.
		--metadata|-m # Metadata component names to convert.
		--metadata-dir|-p # The supplied paths can be to a single file (in which case the operation is applied to only one file) or to a folder (in which case the operation is applied to all metadata types in the directory and its sub-directories).  If you specify this flag, don’t specify --manifest or --metadata. If the comma-separated list you’re supplying contains spaces, enclose the entire comma-separated list in one set of double quotes.
		--output-dir|-d # Directory to store your files in after they’re converted to source format; can be an absolute or relative path.
		--root-dir|-r # Root directory that contains the Metadata API–formatted metadata.
	project:convert:source # To convert source-formatted files into the metadata format, so that you can deploy them using Metadata API, run this command.
		--api-version # Override the api version used for api requests made by this command
		--flags-dir # Import flag values from a directory.
